42 #uuid "4b2d47c1-0f0c-5722-8778-6f2c3236401b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186523993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064388 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Rate of Change of Volume Using Similar Solids" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064388 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064388 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ca9039f1-d299-55fe-8242-b3a6edcb49e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064437 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064437 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Related Rates With Cones" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064437 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064437 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064437 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064437 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8d3375a4-6e38-5507-bb65-38b3c2679448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064623 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064623 #edb/ref 1069 "q-23069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064623 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064623 #edb/ref 1071 "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$ The diameter of the cone is always $3$ times its height. At what rate is the cone's volume increasing when its height is $6\\,\\text{in}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064623 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80257adf-7e8c-5147-9ca1-4f36f76a17ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064623 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064623 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=5\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $3$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =3h \\\\ 2r & =3h \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{2}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3}{2}h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\pi (6)^{2}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =405\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064964 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064964 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064964 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064964 #edb/ref 1071 "The radius of the base of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h}.$ The height of the cone is always half its radius. At what rate is the cone's volume decreasing when the radius of its base is $12\\,\\text{m}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064964 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "438beae6-a258-51c5-a564-575bb144d947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064964 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064964 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=12\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height of the cone is half its radius. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{r}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{r}{2})=\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (12)^{2}(-4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cone's volume is decreasing at a rate of $288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065179 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065179 #edb/ref 1061 631148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065179 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3221fdfa-82ca-53c8-b3b8-3f64fe8fa9db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065179 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065180 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065180 #edb/ref 1061 631141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065180 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9d699428-5e3b-56fd-9632-aa59ba1c3b22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065180 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065330 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065330 #edb/ref 1061 631140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065330 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "98362ef7-51a3-58ec-b8a6-e61dd3fe2604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065330 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065331 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065331 #edb/ref 1061 631145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065331 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "048a3659-fb35-56bd-968d-481e141e8dc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065331 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065332 #edb/ref 1061 631153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065332 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b587e57d-1015-5614-bb4a-85ca3b85dc4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065332 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064388 #edb/ref 17592